1
00:00:02,535 --> 00:00:10,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Says Bhante, since childhood, I've been trapped in self-hatred, seeing only ugliness, failure and burden in my reflection.

2
00:00:11,799 --> 00:00:14,160
[SPEAKER_00]: People say that I'm too harsh on myself.

3
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,361
[SPEAKER_00]: I know it's true, yet the cycle persists.

4
00:00:18,402 --> 00:00:24,744
[SPEAKER_00]: I shun mirrors even, fear new beginnings and hide myself, convinced I will never be good enough.

5
00:00:25,124 --> 00:00:29,706
[SPEAKER_00]: How does one escape such a prison of self-hatred?

6
00:00:31,107 --> 00:00:31,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Well,

7
00:00:32,359 --> 00:00:55,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, whether it's the self-hatred or self-indulgence, it stems from the mind that's untamed and has had it its own way for too long.

8
00:00:55,069 --> 00:00:58,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever intents

9
00:00:59,302 --> 00:01:04,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Moods or emotions you're going through, the intensity of those things comes from the wildness of the mind.

10
00:01:06,146 --> 00:01:18,193
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like everybody has times or periods or... moods of self-hatred or, as I said, self-aggrandizing, whatever is the opposite of it.

11
00:01:19,674 --> 00:01:27,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they usually, you know, will not necessarily dwell on those things as long as some other people might, for whatever circumstantial reasons.

12
00:01:29,002 --> 00:01:37,383
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to recognize that the problem is not really whether you have thoughts of self-hatred or thoughts that you're the greatest person in the world.

13
00:01:38,024 --> 00:01:50,266
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that the intensity of those thoughts reveals the degree of your mind, how much it's not actually tamed, how much it disregards you.

14
00:01:50,326 --> 00:01:51,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the real problem.

15
00:01:52,326 --> 00:01:57,127
[SPEAKER_00]: So, self-loathing, self-hatred is not something that you've

16
00:01:58,121 --> 00:02:00,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a symptom.

17
00:02:00,563 --> 00:02:01,103
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom.

18
00:02:01,624 --> 00:02:16,756
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're reaching the point of, like, chronic, what that person seems to describe, like, chronic self-loathing, it means, like, chronic untameness of its mind, that just keeps picking that for whatever reason.

19
